            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Diethyl carbonate (sometimes abbreviated DEC) is an ester of carbonic acid and ethanol with the formula OC(OCH2CH3)2.  At room temperature (25 °C) diethyl carbonate is a colorless liquid with a low flash point.
Diethyl carbonate is used as a solvent such as in erythromycin intramuscular injections. It can be used as a component of electrolytes in lithium batteries.  It has been proposed as a fuel additive to support cleaner diesel fuel combustion because its high boiling point might reduce blended fuels' volatility, minimizing vapor buildup in warm weather that can block fuel lines. As a fuel additive, it can reduce emissions such as volatile organic compounds, CO2, and particulates."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H226 (97.1%): Flammable liquid and vapor [Warning Flammable liquids]"),
            uses: ["Conductive agent", "Paper Manufacturing"],
            logp: Some(1.179),
            appearance: Some("Diethyl carbonate appears as a colorless liquid with a mild pleasant odor. It is slightly less dense than water and insoluble in water. Hence floats on water. Flash point 77 °F. Vapors are heavier than air. When heated to high temperatures it may emit acrid smoke. Used as a solvent."),
            synonyms: ["105-58-8", "Ethyl carbonate", "Carbonic acid diethyl ester", "Eufin", "Carbonic acid, diethyl ester", "Diatol", "Diaethylcarbonat", "Carbonic ether"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=26028780"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/7766"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_173463"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q420616"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C105588&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    2335: (
            id: 2335,
            name: "N-Methyldiethanolamine",
            formula: "C5H13NO2",
            state: Liquid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 119.0946,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(-21.0),
            boiling_point_c: Some(247.05),
            density_g_cm3: Some(1.038),
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: Some(0.8052),
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Methyldiethanolamine, also known as N-methyl diethanolamine and more commonly as MDEA, is the organic compound with the formula CH3N(C2H4OH)2. It is a colorless liquid with an ammonia odor. It is miscible with water, ethanol and benzene. A tertiary amine, it is widely used as a sweetening agent in chemical, oil refinery, syngas production and natural gas.
Similar compounds are monoethanolamine (MEA), a primary amine, and diethanolamine (DEA), a secondary amine, both of which are also used for amine gas treating. MDEA's defining characteristic when compared to these other amines is its ability to preferentially remove H2S (and strip CO2) from sour gas streams.
MDEA's popularity as a solvent for gas treating stems from several advantages it has when compared to other alkanolamines. One of these advantages is a low vapor pressure, which allows for high amine compositions without appreciable losses through the absorber and regenerator. MDEA is also resistant to thermal and chemical degradation and is largely immiscible with hydrocarbons. MDEA is a common base note in perfumes to allow the fragrance to last. Lastly, MDEA has a relatively low heat of reaction with hydrogen sulfide and carbon dioxide, which allows for lower reboiler duties, thus lower operating costs."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: ["Absorbent", "Adhesive Manufacturing", "All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Intermediate"],
            logp: Some(-1.097),
            appearance: Some("Colorless liquid"),
