            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: Some(0.5162),
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Butane () is an alkane with the formula C4H10. Butane exists as two isomers, n-butane with connectivity CH3CH2CH2CH3 and iso-butane with the formula (CH3)3CH.  Both isomers are highly flammable, colorless, easily liquefied gases that quickly vaporize at room temperature and pressure. Butanes are a trace components of natural gases (NG). The other hydrocarbons in NG include propane, ethane, and especially methane, which are more abundant.  Liquefied petroleum gas is a mixture of propane and some butanes.
The name butane comes from the root but- (from butyric acid, named after the Greek word for butter) and the suffix -ane (for organic compounds)."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H220: Extremely flammable gas [Danger Flammable gases]"),
            uses: ["Cleaning and Furnishing Care Products", "CIR ingredient: Butane", "CIR ingredient: n-Butane"],
            logp: None,
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["E943a", "R-600", "E 943a", "E-943a", "diethyl", "n-Butan", "n-C4H10", "N-BUTANE"],
