            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("4-Aminophenol (or para-aminophenol or p-aminophenol) is an organic compound with the formula H2NC6H4OH. Typically available as a white powder, it is commonly used as a developer for black-and-white film, marketed under the name Rodinal.
Reflecting its slightly hydrophilic character, the white powder is moderately soluble in alcohols and can be recrystallized from hot water. In the presence of a base, it oxidizes readily. The methylated derivatives N-methylaminophenol and N,N-dimethylaminophenol are of commercial value.
The compound is one of three isomeric aminophenols, the other two being 2-aminophenol and 3-aminophenol."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H334: May cause allergy or asthma symptoms or breathing difficulties if inhaled [Danger Sensitization, respiratory]"),
            uses: ["All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "CIR ingredient: p-Aminophenol", "Cyclic Crude and Intermediate Manufacturing"],
            logp: Some(0.974),
            appearance: Some("P-aminophenol appears as white or reddish-yellow crystals or light brown powder. Turns violet when exposed to light. (NTP, 1992)"),
            synonyms: ["aminophenol", "p-Aminophenol", "4-Aminobenzenol", "4-Hydroxyaniline", "p-hydroxyaniline", "p-hydroxyphenylamine", "1-amino-4-hydroxybenzene", "4-amino-1-hydroxybenzene"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=5751925"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/403"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_17602"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2548040"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C123308&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
