            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsMetal, IsAcid],
            description: Some("Sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) or sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS), sometimes written sodium laurilsulfate, is an organic compound with the formula CH3(CH2)11OSO3Na and structure H3C−(CH2)11−O−S(=O)2−O−Na+. It is an anionic surfactant used in many cleaning and hygiene products. This compound is the sodium salt of the 12-carbon organosulfate. Its hydrocarbon tail combined with a polar \"headgroup\" give the compound amphiphilic properties that make it useful as a detergent.  SDS is also component of mixtures produced from inexpensive coconut and palm oils. SDS is a common component of many domestic cleaning, personal hygiene and cosmetic, pharmaceutical, and food products, as well as of industrial and commercial cleaning and product formulations."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H228 (38.9%): Flammable solid [Danger Flammable solids]"),
            uses: ["Cleaning agent", "Emulsifier", "Pigment", "Processing aids, not otherwise listed", "Solvents (for cleaning or degreasing)"],
            logp: Some(3.346),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["SDS", "SLS", "NaDS", "akyposal SDS", "Laurylsiran sodny", "Sodium laurilsulfate", "Sodium dodecylsulfate", "Sodium lauryl sulfate"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=54201"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/6330930"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_8984"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q422241"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C151213&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    3841: (
            id: 3841,
            name: "Potassium cyanide",
            formula: "KCN",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 65.116,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(634.45),
            boiling_point_c: Some(624.85),
            density_g_cm3: Some(1.52),
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.8984),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsMetal],
            description: Some("Potassium cyanide is a compound with the formula KCN. It is a colorless salt, similar in appearance to sugar, that is highly soluble in water. Most KCN is used in gold mining, organic synthesis, and electroplating. Smaller applications include chemical gilding and buffing of jewelry. Potassium cyanide is highly toxic, and a dose of 200 to 300 milligrams will kill nearly any human.
The moist solid emits small amounts of hydrogen cyanide due to hydrolysis (reaction with water). Hydrogen cyanide is often described as having an odor resembling that of bitter almonds.
The taste of potassium cyanide has been described as acrid and bitter, with a burning sensation similar to lye. However, potassium cyanide kills so rapidly its taste has not been reliably documented. In 2006, an Indian man killed himself using potassium cyanide. In the suicide note he left, the final words written were that potassium cyanide \"burns the tongue and tastes acrid\"."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H300+H310+H330 (34.8%): Fatal if swallowed, in contact with skin or if inhaled [Danger Acute toxicity, oral; acute toxicity, dermal; acute toxicity, inhalation]"),
            uses: ["Fabricated Metal Product Manufacturing", "Other (requires additional information)", "PC Code(s): 599600, 0 labels match"],
            logp: Some(-0.364),
            appearance: Some("Potassium cyanide appears as white amorphous lumps or a crystalline mass with a faint odor of bitter almonds. Density 1.52 g / cm3 Toxic by skin absorption through open wounds, by ingestion. Heating to decomposition produces toxic fumes. Used for gold and silver extraction, in chemical analysis, to make other chemicals, and as an insecticide."),
            synonyms: ["KCN", "Zyankali", "Kaliumcyanid", "Kaliumzyanid", "cyanide of potassium", "potassium salt of hydrocyanic acid", "151-50-8", "Potassium cyanide (K(CN))"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=627009"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/9032"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_33191"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q192470"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C151508&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    3842: (
            id: 3842,
            name: "Halothane",
            formula: "C2HBrClF3",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 195.8902,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(118.0),
            boiling_point_c: Some(50.2),
            density_g_cm3: Some(1.871),
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.6585),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Halothane (Bromochlorotrifluoroethane), sold under the brand name Fluothane among others, is a halocarbon with the chemical formula CF3CHBrCl. It is used as a general anaesthetic given by inhalation. It can be used to induce or maintain anaesthesia. Its use in developed countries has been mostly replaced by newer anesthetic agents such as sevoflurane. One of its benefits is that it does not increase the production of saliva, which can be particularly useful in those who are difficult to intubate.
Side effects include an irregular heartbeat, respiratory depression, and hepatotoxicity. Like all volatile anesthetics, it should not be used in people with a personal or family history of malignant hyperthermia. It appears to be safe in porphyria. It is unclear whether its usage during pregnancy is harmful to the fetus, and its use during a C-section is generally discouraged. Halothane is a chiral molecule that is used as a racemic mixture.
Halothane was discovered in 1951. It was approved for medical use in the United States in 1958. It was removed from the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines in 2025. It is not available in the United States. Halothane may contribute to ozone depletion."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(2.3),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["151-67-7", "2-BROMO-2-CHLORO-1,1,1-TRIFLUOROETHANE", "Fluothane", "Narcotane", "Rhodialothan", "Narcotan", "Anestan", "Ftorotan"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=142523"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C151677&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
