    3891: (
            id: 3891,
            name: "Capecitabine",
            formula: "C15H22FN3O6",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 359.1493,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(115.5),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.8595),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Capecitabine, sold under the brand name Xeloda among others, is an anticancer medication used to treat breast cancer, gastric cancer and colorectal cancer. For breast cancer it is often used together with docetaxel. It is taken by mouth.
Common side effects include abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, weakness, and rashes. Other severe side effects include blood clotting problems, allergic reactions, heart problems such as cardiomyopathy, and low blood cell counts. Use during pregnancy may result in harm to the fetus. Capecitabine, inside the body, is converted to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) through which it acts. It belongs to the class of medications known as fluoropyrimidines, which also includes 5-FU and tegafur.
Capecitabine was patented in 1992 and approved for medical use in 1998. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(0.4),
            appearance: Some("gas"),
            synonyms: ["154361-50-9", "Xeloda", "Capecitibine", "Capiibine", "Captabin", "5'-deoxy-5-fluoro-N-[(pentyloxy)carbonyl]cytidi…", "capecitabina", "Capecytabine"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=2073095"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C154361509&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    3892: (
            id: 3892,
            name: "Efavirenz",
            formula: "C14H9ClF3NO2",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 315.0274,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(140.0),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.798),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Efavirenz (EFV), sold under the brand names Sustiva among others, is an antiretroviral medication used to treat and prevent HIV/AIDS. It is generally recommended for use with other antiretrovirals. It may be used for prevention after a needlestick injury or other potential exposure. It is sold both by itself and in combination as efavirenz/emtricitabine/tenofovir. It is taken by mouth.
Common side effects include rash, nausea, headache, feeling tired, and trouble sleeping. Some of the rashes may be serious such as Stevens–Johnson syndrome. Other serious side effects include depression, thoughts of suicide, liver problems, and seizures. It is not safe for use during pregnancy. It is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) and works by blocking the function of reverse transcriptase.
Efavirenz was approved for medical use in the United States in 1998, and in the European Union in 1999. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines. As of 2016, it is available as a generic medication."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(4.6),
