    4292: (
            id: 4292,
            name: "Lenalidomide",
            formula: "C13H13N3O3",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 259.0957,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: None,
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.8591),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Lenalidomide, sold under the brand name Revlimid among others, is a medication used to treat multiple myeloma, smoldering myeloma, various indolent lymphomas, and myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS). For multiple myeloma, it is a first-line treatment, and is given with dexamethasone. It is taken by mouth.
Common side effects include diarrhea, itchiness, joint pain, fever, headache, and trouble sleeping. Severe side effects include low blood platelets, low white blood cells, and blood clots. The dose may need to be adjusted in people with kidney problems.  Lenalidomide is closely related to thalidomide, which is known to cause severe birth defects, so its use during pregnancy is very likely to harm the fetus.
Lenalidomide belongs to a class of drugs known as immunomodulatory imide drugs (IMiDs) or Cereblon E3 ligase modulators, which includes thalidomide and its analogs. In lymphocytes, these drugs target an E3 ubiquitin ligase and change its specificity to include new targets. This results in the rapid degradation of several disease-related proteins including IKZF1, IKZF3, and CSNK1A1.
Lenalidomide was approved for medical use in the United States in 2005. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(-0.4),
            appearance: Some("white, gas"),
            synonyms: ["191732-72-6", "Revlimid", "Revimid", "CC-5013", "CDC 501", "CDC-501", "Lenalidomida", "DTXSID8046664"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1895805"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C191732726&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    4294: (
            id: 4294,
            name: "Prazosin",
            formula: "C19H21N5O4",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 383.1594,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(279.0),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.8725),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Prazosin, sold under the brand name Minipress among others, is a medication used to treat high blood pressure, symptoms of an enlarged prostate, and nightmares related to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). It is an α1 blocker. It is a less preferred treatment of high blood pressure. Other uses may include heart failure and Raynaud syndrome. It is taken by mouth.
Common side effects include dizziness, sleepiness, nausea, and heart palpitations. Serious side effects may include low blood pressure with standing and depression. Prazosin is a non-selective inverse agonist of the α1-adrenergic receptors. It works to decrease blood pressure by dilating blood vessels and helps with an enlarged prostate by relaxing the outflow of the bladder. How it works in PTSD is not entirely clear.
Prazosin was patented in 1965 and came into medical use in 1974. It is available as a generic medication. In 2021, it was the 183rd most commonly prescribed medication in the United States, with more than 2 million prescriptions."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(1.3),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["19216-56-9", "Furazosin", "Prazosine", "Prazosina", "Prazosinum", "XM03YJ541D", "DTXSID4049082", "CP-122991"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=2257429"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C19216569&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
