            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Methylchloroisothiazolinone, also referred to as MCI, is the organic compound with the formula S(C2HCl)C(O)N(CH3). It is a white solid that melts near room temperature. The compound is an isothiazolinone, a class of heterocycles used as biocides. These compounds have an active sulphur moiety that is able to oxidize thiol-containing residues, thereby effectively killing most aerobic and anaerobic bacteria. MCI is often used in combination with methylisothiazolinone, a mixture known as Kathon. The isothiazolinones have attracted attention because they can cause contact dermatitis. Methylchloroisothiazolinone is effective against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, yeast, and fungi."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H300 (20.2%): Fatal if swallowed [Danger Acute toxicity, oral]"),
            uses: ["Preservative", "CIR ingredient: Methylchloroisothiazolinone"],
            logp: Some(1.1),
            appearance: Some("Liquid"),
            synonyms: ["MCI", "CMIT", "chloromethylisothiazolinone", "methylchloroisothiazolinone", "5-chloro-2-methyl-2H-isothiazol-3-one", "5-chloro-2-methyl-1,2-thiazol-3(2H)-one", "2,3-dihydro-2-methyl-3-oxo-5-chloroisothiazole", "26172-55-4"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=49284"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/33344"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_53621"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q204121"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C26172554&Units=SI")],
