            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Potassium chlorate is the inorganic compound with the molecular formula KClO3. In its pure form, it is a white solid. After sodium chlorate, it is the second most common chlorate in industrial use. It is a strong oxidizing agent and its most important application is in safety matches. In other applications it is mostly obsolete and has been replaced by safer alternatives in recent decades. It has been used

in fireworks, propellants and explosives,
to prepare oxygen, both in the lab and in chemical oxygen generators,
as a disinfectant, for example in dentifrices and medical mouthwashes,
in agriculture as a herbicide."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H271: May cause fire or explosion; strong Oxidizer [Danger Oxidizing liquids; Oxidizing solids]"),
            uses: ["Oxidizing agent", "CIR ingredient: Potassium Chlorate"],
            logp: Some(-3.316),
            appearance: Some("Potassium chlorate appears as a white crystalline solid. Forms a very flammable mixture with combustible materials. Mixture may be explosive if combustible material is very finely divided. Mixture may be ignited by friction. Contact with strong sulfuric acid may cause fires or explosions. May spontaneously decompose and ignite when mixed with ammonium salts. May explode under prolonged exposure to heat or fire. Used to make matches, paper, explosives, and many other uses."),
            synonyms: ["ClO3(-)", "chlorate", "[ClO3](-)", "CHLORATE ION", "trioxidochlorate(1-)", "3811-04-9", "Potcrate", "Anforstan"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=302358"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/24187813"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_49709"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q309328"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C3811049&Units=SI")],
