            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Tetrafluoromethane, also known as carbon tetrafluoride or R-14, is the simplest perfluorocarbon (CF4). As its IUPAC name indicates, tetrafluoromethane is the perfluorinated counterpart to the hydrocarbon methane. It can also be classified as a haloalkane or halomethane. Tetrafluoromethane is a useful refrigerant but also a potent greenhouse gas. Although its atmospheric concentration is small, it persists in the atmosphere for 50,000 years, giving it a very high global warming potential. It has a very high bond strength due to the nature of the carbon–fluorine bond."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H280 (100%): Contains gas under pressure; may explode if heated [Warning Gases under pressure]"),
            uses: ["Binder", "All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Low temperature refrigerant; gaseous insulator.", "Oxidizing/reducing agents"],
            logp: Some(1.476),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["F-", "CF4", "F(-)", "PFC-14", "Fluoride", "Freon 14", "Halon 14", "FLUORIDE ION"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=5627714"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/6393"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_17051"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q423055"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C51311172&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    6167: (
            id: 6167,
            name: "Tizanidine",
            formula: "C9H8ClN5S",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 253.0189,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: None,
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.9489),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Tizanidine, sold under the brand name Zanaflex among others, is an alpha-2 (α2) adrenergic receptor agonist, similar to clonidine, that is used to treat muscle spasticity due to spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, and spastic cerebral palsy. Effectiveness appears similar to baclofen or diazepam. It is taken by mouth.
Common side effects of tizanidine include dry mouth, sleepiness, weakness, and dizziness. Serious side effects may include low blood pressure, liver problems, psychosis, and QT prolongation. It is unclear if use in pregnancy and breastfeeding is safe. It is an α2-adrenergic agonist, but how it works is not entirely clear.
Tizanidine was approved for medical use in the United States in 1996. It is available as a generic medication. In 2023, it was the 81st most commonly prescribed medication in the United States, with more than 8 million prescriptions."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(1.4),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["4-Chloro-N-(4,5-dihydro-1H-imidazol-2-yl)-8-thi…", "51322-75-9", "Sirdalud", "Tizanidina", "Tizanidinum", "6AI06C00GW", "DTXSID9023679", "DTXCID203679"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=2858056"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C51322759&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    6168: (
            id: 6168,
            name: "Budesonide",
            formula: "C25H34O6",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 430.5,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(226.0),
            boiling_point_c: Some(326.55),
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.6683),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Budesonide, sold under the brand name Pulmicort, among others, is a steroid medication. It is available as an inhaler, nebulization solution, pill, nasal spray, and rectal foam. The inhaled form is used in the long-term management of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). The nasal spray is used for allergic rhinitis and nasal polyps. Modified-release pills or capsules and rectal forms may be used for inflammatory bowel disease including Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, and microscopic colitis.
Common side effects with the inhaled form include respiratory infections, cough, and headaches. Common side effects with the pills include feeling tired, vomiting, and joint pains. Serious side effects include an increased risk of infection, loss of bone strength, and cataracts. Long-term use of the pill form may cause adrenal insufficiency. Stopping the pills suddenly following long-term use may therefore be dangerous. The inhaled form is generally safe in pregnancy. Budesonide chiefly acts as a glucocorticoid.
Budesonide was initially patented in 1973. Commercial use as an asthma medication began in 1981. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines. Some forms are available as a generic medication. In 2023, it was the 162nd most commonly prescribed medication in the United States, with more than 3 million prescriptions."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H334 (47.5%): May cause allergy or asthma symptoms or breathing difficulties if inhaled [Danger Sensitization, respiratory]"),
            uses: ["Calculated removal (%): 49.8", "MEDICATION", "Use (kg) in USA (2002): 89"],
            logp: Some(2.717),
            appearance: Some("Solid"),
            synonyms: ["11beta,21-dihydroxy-16alpha,17alpha-(butane-1,1…", "(11beta,16alpha)-16,17-(Butylidenebis(oxy))-11,…", "(11beta,16alpha)-16,17-[butylidenebis(oxy)]-11,…", "BUD", "Pulmicort", "51333-22-3", "Rhinocort", "Entocort"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=30862594"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/2462"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_3207"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q422212"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C51333223&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    6169: (
            id: 6169,
            name: "Metoprolol",
            formula: "C15H25NO3",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 267.1834,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(120.0),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.7021),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
