    6729: (
            id: 6729,
            name: "Folic acid",
            formula: "C19H19N7O6",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 441.4,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(250.0),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: Some(1.6),
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.9463),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsAcid, IsBase],
            description: Some("Folate, also known as vitamin B9 and folacin, is one of the B vitamins. Manufactured folic acid, which is converted into folate by the body, is used as a dietary supplement and in food fortification as it is more stable during processing and storage. Folate is required for the body to make DNA and RNA and metabolise amino acids necessary for cell division and maturation of blood cells. As the human body cannot make folate, it is required in the diet, making it an essential nutrient. It occurs naturally in many foods. The recommended adult daily intake of folate in the U.S. is 400 micrograms from foods or dietary supplements.
Folate in the form of folic acid is used to treat anemia caused by folate deficiency. Folic acid is also used as a supplement by women during pregnancy to reduce the risk of neural tube defects (NTDs) in the baby. NTDs include anencephaly and spina bifida, among other defects. Low levels in early pregnancy are believed to be the cause of more than half of babies born with NTDs. More than 80 countries use either mandatory or voluntary fortification of certain foods with folic acid as a measure to decrease the rate of NTDs. Long-term supplementation with relatively large amounts of folic acid is associated with a small reduction in the risk of stroke and an increased risk of prostate cancer.
Not consuming enough folate can lead to folate deficiency. This may result in a type of anemia in which red blood cells become abnormally large. Symptoms may include feeling tired, heart palpitations, shortness of breath, open sores on the tongue, and changes in the color of the skin or hair. Folate deficiency in children may develop within a month of poor dietary intake. In adults, normal total body folate is between 10 and 30 mg with about half of this amount stored in the liver and the remainder in blood and body tissues. In plasma, the natural folate range is 150 to 450 nM.
Folate was discovered between 1931 and 1943. It is on the World Health Organization's List of Essential Medicines. In 2023, it was the 94th most commonly prescribed medication in the United States, with more than 7 million prescriptions. The term \"folic\" is from the Latin word folium (which means leaf) because it was found in dark-green leafy vegetables."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: ["Calculated removal (%): 22", "Food additive"],
            logp: Some(-0.093),
            appearance: Some("Folic acid appears as odorless orange-yellow needles or platelets. Darkens and chars from approximately 482 °F."),
            synonyms: ["PGA", "Acfol", "Folate", "PteGlu", "Folicet", "Folsaeure", "vitamin M", "vitamin B9"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=54117"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/135444779"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_27470"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q110077825"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C59303&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    6730: (
            id: 6730,
            name: "Chloropyramine",
            formula: "C16H20ClN3",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 289.1346,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(25.0),
            boiling_point_c: Some(154.5),
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.7609),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Chloropyramine is a first-generation antihistamine drug approved in several Eastern European countries such as Russia for the treatment of allergic conjunctivitis, allergic rhinitis, bronchial asthma, and other atopic (allergic) conditions. Related indications for clinical use include angioedema, allergic reactions to insect bites, food and drug allergies, and anaphylactic shock.
Chloropyramine is known as a competitive reversible H1 receptor antagonist (also known as an H1 inverse agonist), meaning that it exerts its pharmacological action by competing with histamine for the H1 subtype histamine receptor. By blocking the effects of histamine, the drug inhibits the vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and tissue edema associated with histamine release in the tissue. The H1 antagonistic properties of chloropyramine can be used by researchers for the purposes of blocking the effects of histamine on cells and tissues. In addition, chloropyramine has some anticholinergic properties.
Chloropyramine's anticholinergic properties and the fact that it can pass through the blood–brain barrier are linked to its clinical side effects such as drowsiness, weakness, vertigo, fatigue, dry mouth, constipation, and rarely — visual disturbances and increase of intraocular pressure."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(3.303),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["Halopyramine", "Chlorpyramine", "59-32-5", "Chlorneoantergan", "Anaphylline", "Avapena", "Allergan S", "Chloroneoantergan"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=3154088"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C59325&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
