    6732: (
            id: 6732,
            name: "Phenylephrine",
            formula: "C9H13NO2",
            state: Solid,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 167.0946,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(142.5),
            boiling_point_c: None,
            density_g_cm3: None,
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: Some(0.7535),
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: None,
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: None,
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Phenylephrine, sold under the brand names Neosynephrine and Sudafed PE among others, is a medication used as a decongestant for uncomplicated nasal congestion in the form of a nasal spray or oral tablet, it may be given intravenously in cases of low blood pressure, or used to relieve hemorrhoids as a suppository. It can also be applied to the skin.
Common side effects when taken by mouth or injected include nausea, vomiting, headache, and anxiety. Use on hemorrhoids is generally well tolerated. Severe side effects may include a slow heart rate, intestinal ischemia, chest pain, kidney failure, and tissue death at the site of injection. It is unclear whether its use during pregnancy and breastfeeding is safe. Phenylephrine is a selective α1-adrenergic receptor agonist with minimal to no β-adrenergic receptor agonist activity or induction of norepinephrine release. It causes constriction of both arteries and veins.
Phenylephrine was patented in 1933 and came into medical use in 1938. It is available as a generic medication. Unlike pseudoephedrine, abuse of phenylephrine is very uncommon. Its effectiveness as an oral nasal decongestant has been questioned. In 2023, a U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) panel concluded that the drug was ineffective as a nasal decongestant when taken orally, performing no better than placebo. In November 2024, the FDA proposed to remove oral phenylephrine as an active ingredient that can be used in over-the-counter (OTC) monograph drug products for the temporary relief of nasal congestion."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(-0.31),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["59-42-7", "Metasynephrine", "m-Oxedrine", "Mezaton", "m-Sympatol", "Metasympatol", "Mesatone", "m-Sympathol"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1308957"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C59427&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
