            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsAcid],
            description: Some("Acetamide (systematic name: ethanamide) is an organic compound with the formula CH3CONH2. It is an amide derived from ammonia and acetic acid. It finds some use as a plasticizer and as an industrial solvent. The related compound N,N-dimethylacetamide (DMA) is more widely used, but it is not prepared from acetamide. Acetamide can be considered an intermediate between acetone, which has two methyl (CH3) groups either side of the carbonyl (CO), and urea which has two amide (NH2) groups in those locations. Acetamide is also a naturally occurring mineral with the IMA symbol: Ace."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H351: Suspected of causing cancer [Warning Carcinogenicity]"),
            uses: ["MEDICATION"],
            logp: Some(-0.508),
            appearance: Some("Acetamide appears as colorless crystals with a mousy odor (NTP, 1999). Low toxicity."),
            synonyms: ["Acetamid", "Azetamid", "CH3CONH2", "Ethanamid", "ethanamide", "Essigsaeureamid", "methylformamide", "acetic acid amide"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=870586"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/178"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_27856"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q421721"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C60355&Units=SI")],
