            description: Some("Calcium hydride is the chemical compound with the formula CaH2, an alkaline earth hydride. This grey powder (white if pure, which is rare) reacts vigorously with water, liberating hydrogen gas. CaH2 is thus used as a drying agent, i.e. a desiccant.
CaH2 is a saline hydride, meaning that its structure is salt-like. The alkali metals and the alkaline earth metals heavier than beryllium all form saline hydrides. A well-known example is sodium hydride, which crystallizes in the NaCl motif. These species are insoluble in all solvents with which they do not react. CaH2 crystallizes in the PbCl2 (cotunnite) structure."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H260: In contact with water releases flammable gases which may ignite spontaneously [Danger Substances and mixtures which in contact with water, emit flammable gases]"),
            uses: [],
            logp: Some(-0.916),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["H(-)", "hydride", "hydrogen anion", "CAS Registry Number: 7789-78-8"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=4623969"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/105052"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_29239"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q422847"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C7789788&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
