            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Ethylene thiourea (ETU) is an organosulfur compound with the formula C3H6N2S. It is an example of an N,N-disubstituted thiourea.  It is a white solid.  It is synthesized by treating ethylenediamine with carbon disulfide.
Ethylene thioureas are an excellent accelerant of vulcanization of neoprene rubbers.  In commercial use is the N,N'-diphenylethylenethiourea. Due to reproductive toxicity, carcinogenicity, and mutagenicity, alternatives are being sought to the ethylenethioureas.  One candidate replacement is N-methyl-2-thiazolidinethione.
Ethylene thiourea can be used as a biomarker of exposure to ethylenebisdithiocarbamates (EBDTCs), which are frequently employed as fungicides in agriculture, mainly on fruits, vegetables and ornamental plants."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H360D ***: May damage the unborn child [Danger Reproductive toxicity]"),
            uses: ["Polymerization promoter", "Chemical reaction regulator"],
            logp: Some(-0.536),
            appearance: Some("Ethylene thiourea appears as white to pale green crystals or an off-white solid. Odorless when pure, but technical product may have an amine odor. (NTP, 1992)"),
            synonyms: ["ETU", "Ethylene thiourea", "N,N-Ethylenethiourea", "2-Mercaptoimidazoline", "1,3-Ethylene-2-thiourea", "2-Imidazolidine-2-thione", "imidazolidine-2-thione", "2-Imidazolidinethione"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=33965170"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/2723650"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_34750"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q408767"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C96457&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
